a black police officer in ohio is  speaking to us about what he says was a  racist incident involving his chief that  later made national headlines this  happened in june surveillance video  captured the chief of the sheffield lake  police department putting a paper with  the words ku klux klan on officer keith  poole's raincoat now paul says this was  only the beginning our lead national  correspondent david bagno traveled to  cleveland to sit down with poole and has  the story good morning to you david good  morning to all of you this officer was  just nine months into his job when this  happened to him this was a police  department that was formed 59 years ago  but they only hired their first black  police officer in 2020  and it was officer keith's pool he says  the discrimination he faced at that  department was part of a pattern that  only became public  when this was caught on camera  this surveillance video captured inside  the sheffield lake ohio police  department caught the then police chief  anthony campos senior placing a paper on  the raincoat of the only black police  officer in his department on it the  words ku klux klan  30 seconds later that officer keith  poole walked into the room  people thought i was laughing about it i  wasn't laughing i was distraught like  what do i do with this man he's sitting  right there smiling really he's like  smiling  and  are you  serious  and then he tells everybody everybody  else to come look and see what he did  what's going through your head  it was so demeaning and destructive to  me i  i know what else to do  if that wasn't enough he sat there  and he took a newspaper  and shaped it into a hat of a cone  and sat it there on his head like this  and said you need to wear one of these  on your next call like a clan yes a  clan's hat  four days after the incident the police  union requested a copy of the video  captured on that city camera and that  put the city on notice  mayor dennis bring confronted chief  campo  the chief was standing in his office and  he made a joke  am i going to get fired over this  and i looked at the lawn director and i  said hand him his paper  he said after 30 years this is what i  get i said you have 10 minutes to get  out of your office now the chief was not  fired he reportedly said he was able to  retire  after 32 years of the department and  eight of those years as chief  are you mr campo that doesn't matter we  wanted to ask the former chief why  but we were asked to leave his property  by a man who looked a lot like him  just want to help people  just want to do my job  we always got to  prove ourselves in this job  being black  being a woman  they all got to prove to themselves  and they fair  keith poole has achieved distinction as  an officer serving 30 years in law  enforcement eight of those years as a  school resource officer  to have this man  poke at that  and these kids see that  and they said what was going on with  officer pool  why is he doing that to him  that's only one man  that tried to do damage  but he will not be able to damage me  poole and his attorney ashley k  sleckfold alleged that there was a  pattern of discrimination orchestrated  by the former chief  poole says when he asked the chief for a  new patrol car the chief sent him this  picture of what he could get  the chief also allegedly posted this  picture of poole with the headline  raccoon reaper and poole claims the  chief publicly posted this picture of a  hot sauce bottle on it  the face of the department's only latino  officer  despite all of that officer poole  chooses to still work at that department  why would you want to stay at that  department  the department is not tony campbell  the department is what we make it  he was just part of the department  now that he's gone now it's gonna be a  department and things have gotten better  things have gotten a lot better  well that's because there's a new police  chief and officer pool seems pretty  happy about that mr poole has filed a  complaint with the ohio civil rights  commission next he and his attorney plan  to file a federal civil rights lawsuit  against the city and the former chief  the former chief has not publicly denied  what happened  the mayor says the chief told him  he was just joking  joking and tone deaf i'm so sick of  people trying to hide behind i was only  kidding right you could see the pain the  humiliation on officer poole i so admire  a that he's telling his story to us  and b that he chose to stay because he  said that's not the whole department  that was just that one one officer but  my heart aches looking at what he had to  go through  from the chief who thinks it's funny and  there are people at home that might be  watching this saying well you can't take  a joke it's just a joke  why is it that people of color have to  laugh at things that cause them pain  pain and in this situation that's  exactly what happened it occurred to me  that no matter how old you are you're  never immune to feeling pain he's 57  he's got six adult kids and he said  david it was so embarrassing for me to  have this story be known by others he  has nothing to be embarrassed about  right there's nothing no doubt david  once again great job thank you so much
 

